description:
'''Ernesto Júlio de Nazareth''' (20 March 1863 – 1 February 1934) was a Brazilian [[composer]] and [[pianist]]. He wrote almost entirely for the [[piano]], drawing on the waltzes and polkas of Rio de Janeiro's salons, carnival music, the playing of ''chorões''{{efn|A ''chorão'' (plural ''chorões'') is a musician who plays [[choro]]. The word literally means “one who cries”, but choro is often lively and is not simply sad music.}} and ''pianeiros'',{{efn|A ''pianeiro'' was a working pianist who played in cafés, shops, cinemas, dances or private parties. The word often implied a freer and more rhythmic style than that of a formal concert pianist.}} and the European repertory he had studied, especially [[Frédéric Chopin|Chopin]]. His piano writing adapted figures from the guitar, [[cavaquinho]]{{efn|The ''cavaquinho'' is a small four-string instrument with a bright sound. In choro and samba, it commonly supplies rhythm and harmony.}} and flute as syncopated accompaniment, lyrical melody and inner counterpoint.{{efn|[[Syncopation]] shifts an accent away from the beat where a listener normally expects it. ''Inner counterpoint'' is a secondary melody heard between the bass and the main melody.}}

Nazareth composed about ninety tangos, forty waltzes and twenty polkas, as well as mazurkas, schottisches,{{efn|The [[schottische]] was a nineteenth-century European couple dance in duple time. In Brazil, a related dance and rhythm became known as the ''[[xote]]''.}} marches and sambas. He rejected the name [[Maxixe (dance)|maxixe]]{{efn|The ''maxixe'' was a fast Brazilian couple dance that developed in Rio de Janeiro during the late nineteenth century. Its close body movement led some members of the upper classes to condemn it as improper.}} for his tangos, although they shared its syncopated rhythms. His scores circulated as [[salon music]]{{efn|Here, ''salon music'' means music intended for performance in private homes and small social rooms, as well as in public venues.}} through cafés, music shops and cinemas, in [[choro|''rodas de choro'']]{{efn|''Roda'' means “circle”. In a ''roda de choro'', musicians gather informally—often seated in a circle—to play choro together, with listeners nearby.}} and on the concert stage.

Pieces such as ''Brejeiro'', ''[[Odeon (Nazareth)|Odeon]]'', ''[[Apanhei-te, cavaquinho]]'' and ''Ameno Resedá'' passed from sheet music to bands, recordings, choro groups and concert pianists. His works have been recorded thousands of times, in settings that run from solo piano to chamber groups and orchestras. The [[Brazilian Academy of Music]] named him patron of chair no. 28.{{efn|The Brazilian Academy of Music divides its membership into numbered chairs. Each chair honors a deceased ''patron'', while an elected living member occupies it.}}
